<phyphox version="1.14" locale="en">
  <title>EMI Survey (magnetic field)</title>
  <category>Environment</category>
  <icon>EMI</icon>
  <description>
        WHAT IT DOES
        Finds live power cables through walls, floors and ceilings.

        HOW
        Any wire with current running through it makes a magnetic field around it - the invisible push electricity makes, measured in microtesla. That field fades fast as you move away. Your phone can measure it. Walk along, watch the number climb, and you have found the cable.

        WHY YOU CARE
        Running network cable alongside power cable is a classic mistake. The power cable's field pushes noise into the data cable, and you get errors that look like a bad cable, a bad port or a bad switch. You will replace all three before anyone suspects the tray it is lying in.

        It also finds live conductors - wires or bars carrying electricity - where nothing is labelled, which happens more often than anyone admits.

        HOW TO RUN IT
        1. Press play and hold the phone flat.
        2. Walk SLOWLY along walls, cable trays, containment (the barriers that box in a row of racks) and floors. The field fades fast, so walking briskly past at arm's length will miss it.
        3. Watch peak hold - it keeps the highest value seen so you do not have to stare at the screen while walking.
        4. Press Reset to start a fresh sweep.

        WHAT TO LOOK AT
        Watch the AC activity number, not the total. AC means alternating current - electricity that flips back and forth many times a second. Here is why it matters: the Earth has its own magnetic field, and every piece of steel in the building bends it. So the total is large and lumpy everywhere and means nothing. But steel does not FLICKER. Alternating current does. So the flickering part is the part that says live cable.

        THE HONEST LIMIT
        Your phone reads its magnetometer about 100 times a second. Mains power - the electricity in the wall sockets - flips 50 or 60 times a second. To measure how fast something repeats you must check MORE THAN TWICE as fast as it changes, and 100 is not more than twice 60.

        So this cannot prove a field is mains by its frequency, and it deliberately shows no frequency reading, because any number it gave you there would be false. It tells you this route is busier than that one. That is genuinely useful, and it is all it claims.
    </description>

  <data-containers>
    <container size="3600">bx</container>
    <container size="3600">by</container>
    <container size="3600">bz</container>
    <container size="3600">babs</container>
    <container size="3600">bt</container>

    <container>cnt</container>
    <container>lastFrom</container>
    <container>winFrom</container>

    <container>bLast</container>
    <container>bxLast</container>
    <container>byLast</container>
    <container>bzLast</container>

    <container size="64">win</container>
    <container>bMean</container>
    <container>bAc</container>

    <container>bMax</container>
    <container>bMin</container>
    <container>bPtp</container>

    <!-- cable depth from how fast the AC field falls off -->
    <container init="0">grabSurface</container>
    <container init="0">grabStandoff</container>
    <container init="0">acSurface</container>
    <container init="0">acStandoff</container>
    <container init="0.1">standoff</container>
    <container>emiRatio</container>
    <container>emiDepth</container>
  </data-containers>

  <input>
    <sensor type="magnetic_field" rate="0">
      <output component="x">bx</output>
      <output component="y">by</output>
      <output component="z">bz</output>
      <output component="abs">babs</output>
      <output component="t">bt</output>
    </sensor>
  </input>

  <views>
    <view label="EMI survey">
      <value label="AC activity (fluctuating part)" unit="uT" size="3" precision="3">
        <input>bAc</input>
      </value>
      <value label="Verdict">
        <input>bAc</input>
        <map max="0.3">quiet - just the sensor talking to itself</map>
        <map max="1">slight activity</map>
        <map max="5">notable - a live conductor is near</map>
        <map>strong - keep copper data cable away and cross at right angles</map>
      </value>
      <separator height="0.5"/>
      <value label="Total field now" unit="uT" size="2" precision="2">
        <input>bLast</input>
      </value>
      <value label="Rolling average" unit="uT" precision="2">
        <input>bMean</input>
      </value>
      <value label="Peak hold" unit="uT" precision="2">
        <input>bMax</input>
      </value>
      <value label="Lowest seen" unit="uT" precision="2">
        <input>bMin</input>
      </value>
      <value label="Peak to peak over the run" unit="uT" precision="2">
        <input>bPtp</input>
      </value>
      <separator height="0.5"/>
      <graph label="Total field" labelX="Time" unitX="s" labelY="Magnetic flux density" unitY="uT" partialUpdate="true">
        <input axis="x">bt</input>
        <input axis="y">babs</input>
      </graph>
      <button label="Reset trace and peak hold">
        <input type="empty"/>
        <output>babs</output>
        <input type="empty"/>
        <output>bt</output>
        <input type="empty"/>
        <output>bx</output>
        <input type="empty"/>
        <output>by</output>
        <input type="empty"/>
        <output>bz</output>
      </button>
    </view>

    <view label="Axes">
      <info label="These show the field split into three directions - Bx, By and Bz - measured from the phone's own point of view, not the room's. Useful for working out which way a field is coming from: rotate the phone and watch which direction gets biggest."/>
      <separator height="0.5"/>
      <value label="Bx" unit="uT" precision="2">
        <input>bxLast</input>
      </value>
      <value label="By" unit="uT" precision="2">
        <input>byLast</input>
      </value>
      <value label="Bz" unit="uT" precision="2">
        <input>bzLast</input>
      </value>
      <separator height="0.5"/>
      <graph label="Axes" labelX="Time" unitX="s" labelY="Flux density" unitY="uT" partialUpdate="true">
        <input axis="x">bt</input>
        <input axis="y">bx</input>
      </graph>
    </view>

    <view label="Cable depth">
      <info label="Roughly how far a live cable sits behind the surface. A cable's field gets weaker the further you are from it, in a known way, so two readings at two distances give the distance. Find the strongest spot first (on the Survey tab), then work here."/>
      <separator height="0.5"/>
      <info label="1. Hold the phone flat against the surface, right over the strongest spot. Let the AC activity settle, then press this."/>
      <button label="1. Measure at the surface">
        <input type="value">1</input>
        <output>grabSurface</output>
      </button>
      <value label="Reading at the surface" unit="uT" precision="3">
        <input>acSurface</input>
      </value>
      <separator height="0.5"/>
      <info label="2. Pull the phone straight back from the surface by the standoff distance below - keep it over the same spot - let it settle, and press this."/>
      <edit label="Standoff - how far you pulled back" unit="m" default="0.1" decimal="true">
        <output>standoff</output>
      </edit>
      <button label="2. Measure one standoff out">
        <input type="value">1</input>
        <output>grabStandoff</output>
      </button>
      <value label="Reading one standoff out" unit="uT" precision="3">
        <input>acStandoff</input>
      </value>
      <separator height="0.5"/>
      <value label="Estimated depth behind the surface" unit="m" size="3" precision="3">
        <input>emiDepth</input>
      </value>
      <value label="How much the field dropped (want clearly above 1)" unit="x" precision="2">
        <input>emiRatio</input>
      </value>
      <value label="Is this trustworthy">
        <input>emiRatio</input>
        <map max="1.1">no - the two readings are too close; pull back further or you are not over a cable</map>
        <map max="4">yes - a clear drop, the depth is a fair estimate</map>
        <map>very close to the surface (or a very short standoff)</map>
      </value>
      <separator height="0.5"/>
      <info label="HONEST LIMITS. This assumes a single straight cable. A normal live-and-neutral pair cancels most of its own field and falls off faster, so it reads SHALLOWER than it is - the method works best on a single fat conductor like a busbar or a feeder. It is most accurate for shallow cables and gets rough as the depth passes the standoff. Pull back by a decent fraction of the likely depth: too small a standoff and the two readings barely differ."/>
    </view>

    <view label="Notes">
      <info label="WATCH THE FLICKER, NOT THE TOTAL"/>
      <info label="The Earth makes its own magnetic field, somewhere between 25 and 65 microtesla depending where you live. Every piece of steel around you bends it - racks, floor tiles, door frames, beams. So the total number is big and lumpy everywhere and tells you nothing about whether a cable is live."/>
      <info label="Steel does not flicker. Alternating current does. So the AC activity number is the one that means something."/>
      <separator height="0.5"/>
      <info label="WALK SLOWLY"/>
      <info label="The field falls away sharply with distance. The sensor is a single point in the phone. Walk past a live cable briskly at arm's length and you will miss it completely, and the screen will look calm and reassuring while you do."/>
      <separator height="0.5"/>
      <info label="WHY THERE IS NO FREQUENCY DISPLAY"/>
      <info label="Because it would lie to you, and this is worth understanding once."/>
      <info label="To measure how fast something repeats, you must check MORE THAN TWICE as fast as it repeats. Your phone reads the magnetometer around 100 times a second. Mains - the electricity in the wall sockets - flips 50 or 60 times a second. That is not fast enough."/>
      <info label="When you check too slowly, you do not get a poor answer, you get a confident wrong one. A field that flips 60 times a second, read only 100 times a second, shows up as a 40-times-a-second wobble that does not exist anywhere in reality. Same reason wagon wheels appear to spin backwards on film."/>
      <info label="So this reports HOW MUCH the field flickers, which is real, and refuses to report how fast, which would not be."/>
      <separator height="0.5"/>
      <info label="WHAT IT CANNOT TELL YOU"/>
      <info label="It measures the field in the air. It does NOT measure the interference that actually ends up inside your data cable. Screening (a metal foil wrap around the cable), the twist in each pair of wires, and how the cable is routed all change that a lot, and none of them show up here."/>
      <info label="So treat a strong reading as a reason to look at the cable route. It is not a measurement of crosstalk - noise leaking from a power cable into a data cable - and it is not proof of bit errors, the flipped 1s and 0s that corrupt data. If you have a link problem, test the link."/>
      <separator height="0.5"/>
      <info label="WORDS THIS USES"/>
      <info label="EMI - electrical interference; unwanted electrical noise."/>
      <info label="Magnetic field - the invisible push electricity and magnets make. Measured in microtesla (uT). The Earth makes about 25 to 65 uT."/>
      <info label="Conductor - a wire or bar that carries electricity."/>
      <info label="AC (alternating current) - electricity that flips back and forth many times a second."/>
      <info label="Mains - the electricity in the wall sockets, which flips 50 or 60 times a second."/>
      <info label="Peak hold - keeps the biggest value it has seen so you do not have to watch."/>
      <info label="Noise floor - the tiny wobble a still sensor shows; readings smaller than this are not real."/>
      <info label="Crosstalk - noise leaking from a power cable into a data cable."/>
      <info label="Screening - a metal wrap around a cable that blocks outside noise."/>
      <info label="Bit errors - flipped 1s and 0s that corrupt data."/>
      <info label="Containment - the barriers that box in a row of racks."/>
      <info label="Bx, By, Bz - the field split into three directions, seen from the phone's own point of view."/>
    </view>
  </views>

  <analysis sleep="0.1">
    <count>
      <input clear="false">babs</input>
      <output clear="false">cnt</output>
    </count>

    <!-- newest sample of every axis -->
    <formula formula="max([1]-1,0)">
      <input clear="false">cnt</input>
      <output>lastFrom</output>
    </formula>
    <subrange>
      <input as="from" clear="false">lastFrom</input>
      <input as="in" clear="false">babs</input>
      <input as="in" clear="false">bx</input>
      <input as="in" clear="false">by</input>
      <input as="in" clear="false">bz</input>
      <output>bLast</output>
      <output>bxLast</output>
      <output>byLast</output>
      <output>bzLast</output>
    </subrange>

    <!-- rolling window for the AC content -->
    <formula formula="max([1]-64,0)">
      <input clear="false">cnt</input>
      <output>winFrom</output>
    </formula>
    <subrange>
      <input as="from" clear="false">winFrom</input>
      <input as="in" clear="false">babs</input>
      <output>win</output>
    </subrange>
    <average>
      <input as="buffer">win</input>
      <output as="average">bMean</output>
      <output as="stddev">bAc</output>
    </average>

    <!-- peak hold over the whole trace -->
    <max>
      <input as="y" clear="false">babs</input>
      <output as="max">bMax</output>
    </max>
    <min>
      <input as="y" clear="false">babs</input>
      <output as="min">bMin</output>
    </min>
    <subtract>
      <input clear="false">bMax</input>
      <input clear="false">bMin</input>
      <output>bPtp</output>
    </subtract>

    <!-- Cable depth. The field of a long cable falls off as 1/distance, so if
         the AC field is B1 against the surface (distance = the depth d) and B2
         one standoff s further out (distance d+s), then B1/B2 = (d+s)/d, which
         solves to d = s/(B1/B2 - 1). We use the AC (fluctuating) part, so the
         Earth's steady field cancels itself out of the ratio. -->
    <if equal="true">
      <input clear="false">grabSurface</input>
      <input type="value">1</input>
      <input clear="false">bAc</input>
      <output>acSurface</output>
    </if>
    <append>
      <input type="value">0</input>
      <output>grabSurface</output>
    </append>
    <if equal="true">
      <input clear="false">grabStandoff</input>
      <input type="value">1</input>
      <input clear="false">bAc</input>
      <output>acStandoff</output>
    </if>
    <append>
      <input type="value">0</input>
      <output>grabStandoff</output>
    </append>
    <formula formula="[1]/max([2],0.0001)">
      <input clear="false">acSurface</input>
      <input clear="false">acStandoff</input>
      <output>emiRatio</output>
    </formula>
    <formula formula="[1]/max([2]-1,0.001)">
      <input clear="false">standoff</input>
      <input clear="false">emiRatio</input>
      <output>emiDepth</output>
    </formula>
  </analysis>

  <export>
    <set name="Field trace">
      <data name="Time (s)">bt</data>
      <data name="Bx (uT)">bx</data>
      <data name="By (uT)">by</data>
      <data name="Bz (uT)">bz</data>
      <data name="Total field (uT)">babs</data>
    </set>
    <set name="Summary">
      <data name="Peak hold (uT)">bMax</data>
      <data name="Lowest (uT)">bMin</data>
      <data name="Peak to peak (uT)">bPtp</data>
      <data name="AC activity (uT)">bAc</data>
    </set>
  </export>
</phyphox>
